details.contract:
app/src/shared/cli/args.ts exports exactly these names and no others:

export interface InputField { readonly type: string; readonly required?: boolean; readonly default?: unknown; readonly min?: number; readonly max?: number; readonly values?: readonly string[]; readonly items?: { readonly type?: string; readonly values?: readonly string[] }; readonly description?: string }
export type InputLiteral = Readonly<Record<string, InputField>>;
export type ToolArgs = Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;
export const GLOBAL_VALUE_FLAGS = ["repo-root", "data-dir"] as const;
export function splitGlobals(argv: readonly string[]): { readonly json: boolean; readonly help: boolean; readonly rest: readonly string[] };
export function parseArgs(tool: string, input: InputLiteral, raw: unknown): ToolArgs;
export function parseCliArgs(tool: string, input: InputLiteral, argv: readonly string[]): ToolArgs;
export function usageOf(tool: string, input: InputLiteral): string;
export class CliUsageError extends Error { readonly usage: string }

Move parseArgs, matchesType, matchesItem and expectationOf verbatim from app/src/index.ts (lines ~232-320) and keep their behaviour identical: unknown keys dropped, declared defaults applied, a missing required argument refused with AppError("internal", `<tool> is missing the required argument "<name>"`), a wrong type refused with AppError("internal", `<tool> received an invalid "<name>" argument`), both with expectationOf as hint. Do not add min/max clamping; handlers own that.

splitGlobals is the one place the CLI's global flags are understood. It scans argv and removes: `--json` (sets json: true), `--help` and `-h` (sets help: true), and `--repo-root`/`--data-dir` in both the `--name value` and `--name=value` forms. Everything else lands in `rest`, in order, untouched. A global value flag given with no value is a CliUsageError naming the flag. Non-global `--flags` stay in rest so parseCliArgs can refuse them.

parseCliArgs receives argv WITHOUT the leading tool token and WITHOUT the global flags — callers pass splitGlobals(argv).rest. It builds the raw record and returns parseArgs(tool, input, raw), converting any AppError into CliUsageError with the same message and usageOf(tool, input) as `usage`. Rules: a token without a leading `--` binds the single field declared `required: true` when that field is of type `string`; a second positional, or any positional for a literal with no such field (`status`, `summary`, `impact`, `index`), is a CliUsageError `unexpected positional argument "<token>"`; `--name value` and `--name=value` are both accepted; a missing value for a flag is a CliUsageError; integer flags are parsed with Number and a non-integer is a CliUsageError; boolean fields accept `--flag`, `--flag=true`, `--flag=false` and `--no-flag`; array fields collect repeated occurrences (`--paths a --paths b`) and never split on commas; an unknown `--flag` is a CliUsageError that names it. `--help`/`-h` never reaches parseCliArgs because splitGlobals removed it; do not special-case it here.

usageOf returns `cgraph <tool> [<required-name>] [options]` followed by one line per field, `  --<name> <type>` then the field's `description` when present, marks required fields, and ends with a line naming the global flags `--repo-root`, `--data-dir` and `--json`. It must mention every field name.

do not edit app/src/index.ts in this task; it keeps its local copies until TASK-008. Do not import from tools or indexer; only node:path/node:fs builtins and #shared/errors are allowed.

app/src/shared/cli/render.ts exports exactly:

export const EXIT_FAILURE = 1;
export const EXIT_USAGE = 2;
export function renderEnvelope(envelope: Envelope<unknown>): string;
export function statusLines(envelope: Envelope<unknown>): string;
export function exitCodeOf(envelope: Envelope<unknown>): number;

renderEnvelope returns JSON.stringify(envelope) + "\n" — compact, one line, no indentation, no extra text.

statusLines reproduces runStatus's human output from app/src/index.ts (~lines 790-806): a first line `<status>: <summary>`, then, only when the envelope carries a payload, four detail lines joined with "\n" and ending with "\n" — `project  <name> (<root>)`, `database <db_path>`, `graph    <nodes> nodes, <edges> edges, <size_bytes> bytes`, `health   <indexer>, <n> consecutive failures`. That is five lines in total when a payload exists and one line when it does not. Fallbacks are exactly runStatus's: a missing `project.name` or `health.indexer` renders `unknown`, a missing `project.root` renders `unrecorded`, and a missing payload omits the four detail lines.

It must accept any envelope and narrow the payload defensively from `unknown` (no import of a tool module): status.summary string, payload.project.{name,root,db_path}, payload.graph.{nodes,edges,size_bytes}, payload.health.{indexer,consecutive_failures}.

exitCodeOf returns 0 for status ok, ambiguous and not_found, and EXIT_FAILURE for error and bootstrapping. EXIT_USAGE is not produced here; callers use it for argv/config refusals.

Only #shared/envelope types may be imported; no tool, no indexer, no filesystem.
